>
  <h2>Step 0: check it is a programming problem</h2>
  <p>Many plateaus are not. Before you rewrite your plan, look at <a href="/why-my-bench-press-is-weak/">technique, recovery and where the bar slows</a>, and consider a <a href="/how-to-deload-bench-press-properly/">deload week</a>. Jordan's signs of a lifter who needs recovery are poor sleep, training that feels like a grind, feeling worn out, and numbers that are trending down or have not moved in a long time.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: add reps, then weight</h2>
  <p>Jordan's progressive overload rule is that if you can do 5 reps with a weight on day one and you are stronger by week four, you add reps or you add weight. Both are overload. If you use the same load forever, your body has no new stress to adapt to. His ranking: first add more reps, then add more weight. In practice, if you did 3 sets of 5 last week, try for 3 sets of 6 at the same weight before you jump the load.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: give the work room to grow</h2>
  <p>Ralston and colleagues pooled nine studies in 2017 and found a graded dose-response between weekly sets and strength: more sets per week per exercise gave larger gains, with high beating low and medium falling in between. If your bench volume has sat unchanged for months, a small, gradual increase is a reasonable thing to test, as long as your recovery and performance hold up. More volume on top of poor recovery will usually make a plateau worse.</p>

  <h2>Step 3: vary the stimulus with a plan</h2>
  <p>Two meta-analyses are worth knowing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Williams and colleagues (2017)</strong> pooled 81 effects from 18 studies and found periodized programs beat non-periodized ones for one rep max in the squat, bench press and leg press (effect size 0.43). Undulating programs were more favorable than linear ones, and higher training frequency and longer studies went with bigger gains.</li>
    <li><strong>Moesgaard and colleagues (2022)</strong> pooled 35 studies where total volume was equal between groups. The advantage for periodized training on one rep max shrank to 0.31. Undulating beat linear periodization overall (0.31), and the gap was larger in trained lifters (0.61, with a wide range of uncertainty) than in untrained ones, where it was nil. Muscle size did not differ between approaches.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For you, that means a trained lifter who is stuck on a flat program may get something from swapping to weekly or daily undulation, for example a heavy day, a volume day and a lighter technique day. The effect is moderate and strongest for strength. The <a href="/rpe-bench-press-program/">RPE bench press program</a> is one way to set it up.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: challenge the nervous system</h2>
  <p>When adding weight every week stops working and you are not overtrained, Jordan's first steps are to slow the lowering phase, or to pause at the bottom of the lift for 3 seconds and then press. See <a href="/controlling-eccentric-on-bench-press-tips/">bench press tempo and eccentric control</a>.</p>

  <h2>Step 5: build and finish a block properly</h2>
  <p>Jordan's description of a realistic 8 week strength progression is a useful template:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Start with higher overall reps and volume. Tempo can supply some of that volume.</li>
    <li>As the weeks go on, the weights get heavier and total volume comes down, with sets possibly going up.</li>
    <li>Toward the end, mix in faster, dynamic lifts.</li>
    <li>At the end of the block, move light weight very fast and move maximal weight for very low reps, potentially through different ranges of motion.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <table class="pct-table data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Weeks</th><th>Example bench work (not a prescription)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1 and 2</td><td class="wrap">4 x 8 with a controlled lowering</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 and 4</td><td class="wrap">4 x 6, heavier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5 and 6</td><td class="wrap">5 x 4, plus a few light, fast sets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7</td><td class="wrap">3 x 2 heavy, plus light, fast sets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td class="wrap">Test a 3 to 5 rep max, which Jordan uses as a stand-in for a one rep max</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Then deload. A survey of 246 competitive lifters (Rogerson and colleagues, 2024) found they deloaded about every 5 to 6 weeks on average, for about 6 days, but that is common practice and not a tested rule.</p>

  <h2>What this evidence cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>The meta-analyses cover many exercises and populations, and most trials are short. None isolates the bench press plateau in an experienced lifter. The order above puts the cheapest, safest changes first, and asks you to change one thing at a time and give each 8 to 12 weeks.</p>
